    "narrative": "Today\u2019s offensive engine is anchored by **Josh Naylor** (78.46) and **James Wood** (69.76). **Josh Naylor** benefits from a high-leverage matchup against Brandon Young; the combination of a favorable park factor (+16%) and the pitcher\u2019s shaky 4.69 xERA creates an ideal environment for multi-hit potential. Meanwhile, **James Wood** remains a priority play despite a difficult matchup against Robbie Ray. His elite .403 xwOBA and the tactical advantage of the leadoff spot (+10%) outweigh the negative impact of Ray\u2019s catching efficiency, ensuring he remains locked in to capitalize on his high-upside profile. **Tyler Soderstrom** and **Seiya Suzuki** also grade out as strong starts (68.74 and 67.36, respectively), with both hitters positioned to exploit favorable pitching matchups and park factors that tilt the projections well above their base levels.\n\nRegarding our stolen base strategy, **Corbin Carroll** (67.03) is a vital component of today\u2019s lineup. While facing Ryan Gusto presents a challenge, the projected \"slow delivery\" of the opposing starter provides a crucial +4% lift to **Corbin Carroll**\u2019s already strong SB utility, making him one of our most efficient producers for a category we view as essential. We also closely monitored **Vinnie Pasquantino** (52.60) on the bench; he was the primary candidate to displace a lower-tier starter, but his substantial penalty for facing a left-handed arm (-15% platoon) ultimately kept him out of the starting nine in favor of higher-floor options like **Austin Riley**.\n\nRisks remain present across the board due to the \"Lineup Pending\" status for most of the roster. Specifically, **Gabriel Moreno**, **Brice Turang**, and **Max Muncy** are dependent on their projected batting orders; should any of these players drop further down the lineup once official cards are exchanged, their efficiency scores will suffer immediate downward pressure. Additionally, users should keep a close watch on **Kyle Karros**, whose score is buoyed by park factors but constrained by the high-skill profile of Shota Imanaga. If the weather reports shift to suppress offense in that specific venue, **Kyle Karros** is the first candidate we would look to swap for more stable bench depth.",
